PG.notes("l134", "<p class=\"summary\">Short preview of the Drum Kit project built across this section. It frames the section goal: reacting to both button clicks and keyboard presses through DOM event listeners.</p>\n<h3>What the Drum Kit does</h3>\n<ul><li>The page shows a row of keys, each standing for one drum in a drum set.</li><li>Clicking a key plays the matching drum sound.</li><li>Pressing the corresponding key on the keyboard plays the same sound, so both input routes share one behaviour.</li><li>The project is the vehicle for intermediate DOM topics: attaching event listeners to buttons and to the keyboard, and responding to what the user did.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l135", "<p class=\"summary\">Admin step: grab the starter files for the Drum Kit project from the lecture's Resources button. No video or substantial article body was readable on the page.</p>\n<h3>Setup</h3>\n<ul><li>Use the Resources dropdown on this lecture to download the Drum Kit starter zip, then extract it and move the folder into the Web Development Projects folder before opening it in VS Code.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's later lessons show the starter assets: index.html, styles.css, an images folder (one image per drum) and a sounds folder (one audio file per drum), with script.js as the file you write.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Opening the still-zipped download in VS Code instead of extracting it first.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l136", "<p class=\"summary\">Wires the Drum Kit page to a script file, then uses addEventListener so a button reacts to clicks. Explains why the handler is passed by name (no parentheses), shows the anonymous-function form, and ends with a challenge to attach the listener to every drum button using a loop.</p>\n<h3>Linking the script and testing</h3>\n<ul><li>The starter project has index.html, styles.css and a JavaScript file (called index.js in the video; script.js in the reference repo). Link it with a script tag with a src attribute placed just before the closing body tag, so the DOM already exists when the code runs.</li><li>Prove the link works by putting a throwaway alert in the JS file and reloading.</li><li>At the start the seven buttons (w, a, s, d, j, k, l) look right but do nothing when clicked.</li></ul>\n<h3>Event listeners: the idea</h3>\n<ul><li>An event listener tells an element to watch for a specific kind of event and run some code when it happens. The element is the target, the event is what it is watching for, and the function is the reaction.</li><li>Step 1: write a function for the reaction (here handleClick, no parameters, which shows an alert).</li><li>Step 2: select the element with document.querySelector (returns only the FIRST match, here the w button).</li><li>Step 3: call addEventListener on that element.</li><li>The MDN page for addEventListener is the place to check argument details; the video stresses reading docs for any new method.</li></ul>\n<h3>addEventListener arguments</h3>\n<ul><li>First argument: the event type as a case-sensitive string, such as &quot;click&quot;. MDN lists every available event type.</li><li>Second argument: the listener, normally a function that the browser calls when the event is detected.</li><li>After the change only the first button responds to clicks; the other buttons still do nothing, which proves the listener is attached per element.</li></ul>\n<h3>Passing the function vs calling it</h3>\n<ul><li>Write handleClick without parentheses. This hands the function over so the browser can call it later, when the click happens.</li><li>With parentheses (handleClick()) the function runs immediately while the script loads, as the line is executed, so the alert fires on page load and nothing useful is registered as the listener.</li><li>This 'pass a function as a value to be called later' idea is a recurring pattern in JavaScript and takes some getting used to.</li></ul>\n<h3>Anonymous functions as listeners</h3>\n<ul><li>Instead of a named function you can write the function inline as the second argument: function () { ... }. It has no name, so it can't be reused elsewhere, but behaves identically here.</li><li>This inline form is the most common style seen in real code.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge solution: loop over all drum buttons</h3>\n<ul><li>querySelectorAll returns a list of every match, indexed from 0, with a length property.</li><li>Count the elements with document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;).length rather than hard-coding 7. Selecting by the drum class is safer than selecting by the button tag, because a later extra button (e.g. a social link in the footer) would otherwise also get the listener.</li><li>Run a for loop from i = 0 while i is below that count, incrementing by 1, and in the body call addEventListener on querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;)[i].</li><li>Storing the count in a variable such as numberOfDrumButtons makes the loop header easier to read.</li><li>A while loop works equally well. The one thing to avoid is copy-pasting seven separate lines.</li><li>If this was hard, revisit the loops lessons from the JavaScript section.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Named handler passed by reference</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function handleClick() {\n  alert(&quot;I got clicked!&quot;);\n}\n\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;.drum&quot;).addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, handleClick); // no ()</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Anonymous function version, applied to every drum button (matches the reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var numberOfDrumButtons = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;).length;\n\nfor (var i = 0; i &lt; numberOfDrumButtons; i++) {\n  document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;)[i].addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n    alert(&quot;I got clicked!&quot;);\n  });\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Add the click listener to every drum button</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Drum Kit starter (index.html with seven buttons of class drum, styles.css). Script is linked, and a listener is currently attached only to the first button via querySelector.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Clicking any of the seven buttons shows the alert 'I got clicked!', using no more than about five lines and no repetition.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Link the JS file before the closing body tag and test with an alert.</li><li>Count the buttons with document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;).length (optionally store in a variable).</li><li>Write a for loop from 0 up to that count.</li><li>Inside the loop select the i-th .drum element with square brackets and call addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, ...) with an anonymous function that alerts.</li><li>Reload and click each button to verify.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> An alert appears for every one of the seven buttons.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var numberOfDrumButtons = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;).length;\n\nfor (var i = 0; i &lt; numberOfDrumButtons; i++) {\n  document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;)[i].addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n    alert(&quot;I got clicked!&quot;);\n  });\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Adding parentheses after the handler name, so it runs at page load instead of on click.</li><li>Using querySelector (first match only) when every element needs the listener.</li><li>Looping over the generic button tag, which will hit unrelated buttons added later.</li><li>Misspelling or mis-casing the event type string.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's index.html has a footer crediting the repo author; ignore it. Its markup also includes the Arvo Google font link used by the styling.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l137", "<p class=\"summary\">Uses Chrome DevTools and a small calculator to show that functions can be passed as arguments to other functions (higher order functions), which is exactly what addEventListener relies on. Ends with a challenge to complete a four-operation calculator and step through it in the debugger.</p>\n<h3>Experimenting on a live page with DevTools</h3>\n<ul><li>Right-click an element, choose Inspect, and the selected element is available in the console as $0.</li><li>Assigning to a property such as $0.innerHTML = &quot;...&quot; changes the text on the page instantly. Only the local copy in your browser changes; a reload restores the original from the server.</li><li>$0.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () { console.log(...) }) attaches a listener to a page you did not write, and clicking the element then logs to the console every time.</li></ul>\n<h3>addEventListener takes a function as input</h3>\n<ul><li>The first input is the event type string; the second is the function to run once that event is detected.</li><li>The second input can be an anonymous function or a named function such as respondToClick.</li><li>Unlike earlier functions that receive strings or numbers, this one receives a function as data. The browser's own implementation is hidden, so the lecture rebuilds the same pattern with a calculator.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why pass a function: the calculator</h3>\n<ul><li>A calculator(num1, num2) that hard-codes + must be edited to switch to *, which is clumsy.</li><li>Better: make small single-purpose functions (add, multiply, and later subtract, divide), each taking two numbers and returning a result.</li><li>Give calculator a third parameter, operator. Inside it, call operator(num1, num2) and return the result.</li><li>At the call site the operator argument is the function's name only, with no parentheses, e.g. calculator(4, 5, add) returns 9 and calculator(4, 5, multiply) returns 20. Passing the name hands over the function; parentheses would call it first and pass its result instead.</li><li>Trace of calculator(4, 5, multiply): 4 binds to num1, 5 to num2, the multiply function binds to operator; the body calls operator with the two numbers; its return value becomes calculator's return value.</li></ul>\n<h3>Higher order functions</h3>\n<ul><li>A function that accepts another function as an argument (like calculator, and like addEventListener) is called a higher order function.</li><li>This is available in many modern languages (JavaScript, Swift, Pascal) but not all. It is used constantly in DOM scripting.</li></ul>\n<h3>Using the Chrome debugger</h3>\n<ul><li>Type the keyword debugger in the console (Shift+Enter for a new line), then a call such as calculator(3, 4, multiply), and execution pauses in the Sources panel.</li><li>Use the step-into button (down arrow) to advance one call at a time. The scope panel shows num1 = 3, num2 = 4 and operator shown as a function (marked with f).</li><li>Stepping shows control enter calculator, jump into multiply, then return 12 back out through both functions.</li><li>Use this when code misbehaves, instead of simulating execution mentally, which can be wrong.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Passing functions into calculator (matches the reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function addition(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 + num2;\n}\nfunction subtraction(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 - num2;\n}\nfunction multiplication(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 * num2;\n}\nfunction division(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 / num2;\n}\n\nfunction calculator(num1, num2, operator) {\n  return operator(num1, num2);\n}\n\nconsole.log(calculator(4, 5, addition));       // 9\nconsole.log(calculator(5, 4, subtraction));    // 1\nconsole.log(calculator(3, 4, multiplication)); // 12\nconsole.log(calculator(10, 2, division));      // 5</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">DevTools experiments</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$0.innerHTML = &quot;New title&quot;;\n$0.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n  console.log(&quot;I got clicked&quot;);\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Complete the four-operation calculator</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A calculator(num1, num2, operator) function plus add and multiply functions, in the console or a script file.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Add divide and subtract so any of the four operations can be selected by passing a function to calculator.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Write add, subtract, multiply and divide, each with two parameters and a return value.</li><li>Write calculator(num1, num2, operator) that returns operator(num1, num2).</li><li>Call it for each operation, passing the function by name (no parentheses), e.g. calculator(6, 3, subtract).</li><li>Use the debugger keyword and step-through to follow one call.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each call returns the right arithmetic result (e.g. 6 and 3 with subtract gives 3).</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function calculator(num1, num2, operator) {\n  return operator(num1, num2);\n}\nconsole.log(calculator(10, 2, division)); // 5</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing calculator(4, 5, add()) which calls add immediately with undefined arguments and passes NaN or a result instead of a function.</li><li>Forgetting the return statement inside calculator, so it yields undefined.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> $0 only refers to the most recently inspected element, and edits made in DevTools vanish on reload.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l138", "<p class=\"summary\">Solution page for the four-operation calculator challenge from the previous lecture. The page body was only an 'Open' link-out, so this note is built from the reference repo's finished code.</p>\n<h3>Solution structure</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Four operator functions (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division) each take num1 and num2 and return the result of one arithmetic operator.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> calculator(num1, num2, operator) is the higher order function: it simply returns operator(num1, num2), so the caller chooses the behaviour by passing a function name.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Call sites pass the function name without parentheses. Adding parentheses would invoke the function early with no arguments.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Division by zero in JavaScript gives Infinity rather than an error; there is no guard in the solution.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function addition(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 + num2;\n}\nfunction subtraction(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 - num2;\n}\nfunction multiplication(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 * num2;\n}\nfunction division(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 / num2;\n}\n\nfunction calculator(num1, num2, operator) {\n  return operator(num1, num2);\n}\n\nconsole.log(calculator(4, 5, addition));       // 9\nconsole.log(calculator(5, 4, subtraction));    // 1\nconsole.log(calculator(3, 4, multiplication)); // 12\nconsole.log(calculator(10, 2, division));      // 5</pre>\n<h2>The task: Higher order function challenge (solution)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> calculator with add and multiply from the previous lecture.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Support subtract and divide as well, selected by passing a function.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Define subtraction and division with two parameters each.</li><li>Call calculator with each of the four function names and log the results.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Logs 9, 1, 12, 5 for the four example calls.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function subtraction(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 - num2;\n}\nfunction division(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 / num2;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Reading this page expecting a long article: the content is a link-out, not text on the page.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l139", "<p class=\"summary\">Plays a drum sound from JavaScript using new Audio and play(), styles each drum button with a background image, and introduces the keyword this to learn which button triggered a listener. Includes three small challenges: play a sound, add button images, and turn the clicked button's text white.</p>\n<h3>Finding and understanding a snippet</h3>\n<ul><li>When unsure how to do something, search plainly (e.g. 'play sound javascript'); a Stack Overflow answer shows the pattern: create an audio object and call play on it.</li><li>Don't paste snippets blindly. Look up each part on MDN to learn what it does before using it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Audio objects</h3>\n<ul><li>new Audio(url) constructs an HTMLAudioElement (a kind of HTMLMediaElement) that points at a sound file. Keep it in a variable.</li><li>The path is relative to the HTML page, so a file in the sounds folder is written as sounds/tom-1.mp3 (the reference repo uses ./sounds/tom-1.mp3).</li><li>Media elements have properties (e.g. currentTime, paused) and methods; audio.play() starts playback.</li><li>Replacing the alert in the click listener with these two lines makes every button play the tom-1 sound. All buttons sound the same until each is tied to its own file (done in the next lesson).</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge: button images</h3>\n<ul><li>The images folder has seven drum pictures (tom1 to tom4, snare, crash, kick). Give each button a background-image in styles.css using url() with a path such as images/tom1.png.</li><li>Assign them to the w, a, s, d, j, k, l button classes in whatever order you like (the course uses tom1-4 for the first four keys, then snare, crash, kick), but remember the order so sounds can be matched later.</li><li>In the reference repo: .w tom1, .a tom2, .s tom3, .d tom4, .j snare, .k crash, .l kick.</li></ul>\n<h3>Identifying the clicked button with this</h3>\n<ul><li>Inside an event listener's function, this refers to the element that triggered the event. console.log(this) prints the button that was clicked.</li><li>Because this is the element, its properties are available: this.innerHTML gives the key letter shown on the button, which can later be used to choose a sound.</li><li>Flow recap: the loop selects every .drum element in turn and attaches a listener; a click on any one calls the function with this set to that button.</li><li>The sound code was commented out while experimenting so only one thing is tested at a time.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge: change style with this</h3>\n<ul><li>this.style.color = &quot;white&quot; changes the text colour of whichever button was clicked, using the same DOM style property seen in earlier lessons.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Play a sound on click</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var numberOfDrumButtons = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;).length;\n\nfor (var i = 0; i &lt; numberOfDrumButtons; i++) {\n  document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;)[i].addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n    var audio = new Audio(&quot;./sounds/tom-1.mp3&quot;);\n    audio.play();\n  });\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Button images (CSS, reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.w { background-image: url(./images/tom1.png); }\n.a { background-image: url(./images/tom2.png); }\n.s { background-image: url(./images/tom3.png); }\n.d { background-image: url(./images/tom4.png); }\n.j { background-image: url(./images/snare.png); }\n.k { background-image: url(./images/crash.png); }\n.l { background-image: url(./images/kick.png); }</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Using this inside the listener</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;)[i].addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n  console.log(this);            // the clicked button\n  console.log(this.innerHTML);  // its letter\n  this.style.color = &quot;white&quot;;   // restyle it\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Sound, images and this</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Drum Kit with the click listener loop from the previous lesson (alert inside).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Clicking a button plays a drum sound, each button shows a drum image, and the clicked button's text turns white.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Replace the alert with new Audio(&quot;sounds/tom-1.mp3&quot;) and a call to .play().</li><li>Add background-image rules for the w, a, s, d, j, k, l classes pointing at the files in images.</li><li>Log this in the listener, click each button and inspect the output.</li><li>Replace the log with this.style.color = &quot;white&quot;.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Every button plays the tom-1 sound for now, shows its drum picture, and (with the last step) its letter turns white after clicking.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var audio = new Audio(&quot;./sounds/tom-1.mp3&quot;);\naudio.play();</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Wrong relative path to the sound or image file, so nothing plays or shows (check the folder name and that the extension matches).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's sounds folder here contains only tom-1.mp3; the full set arrives in the next lessons.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Browsers may block audio until the user has interacted with the page; clicks count as interaction.</li><li>Using the arrow-function form would change what this refers to; the lecture uses a regular function.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l140", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces the switch statement as a tidier alternative to a long if-else chain, then uses it to map each drum button's letter to a different sound file.</p>\n<h3>Why switch</h3>\n<ul><li>With seven possible letters, an if / else if chain would be long and repetitive. A switch picks one branch based on the value of a single expression.</li><li>Editors can autocomplete the switch skeleton when you type the keyword.</li></ul>\n<h3>Switch syntax</h3>\n<ul><li>switch (expression) { ... } evaluates the expression once; here it is a variable holding the clicked button's text.</li><li>Store the value first: var buttonInnerHTML = this.innerHTML; this is the button that fired the listener.</li><li>Each case label is followed by a colon and the statements to run when the value matches (a strict comparison against the case value).</li><li>break ends the case and exits the switch so execution carries on after it. Without it, execution would fall through into the next case.</li><li>default is the catch-all, like a final else. It handles values no case matched, such as an unexpected character.</li><li>Even if default seems unreachable, include it; here it just logs the unexpected value, which helps debugging later.</li></ul>\n<h3>Applying it to the drum kit</h3>\n<ul><li>Cases: w plays tom-1, a tom-2, s tom-3, d tom-4, j snare, k crash, l kick-bass (these match the images chosen in the previous lesson).</li><li>Name each Audio variable after its sound (tom1, tom2, snare...) rather than reusing the name audio, and call play() on the same variable you created.</li><li>After saving, each button now plays a different sound.</li><li>Next lesson adds keyboard events so keys trigger the same sounds.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Switch inside the click listener (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var numberOfDrumButtons = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;).length;\n\nfor (var i = 0; i &lt; numberOfDrumButtons; i++) {\n  document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;)[i].addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n    var buttonInnerHTML = this.innerHTML;\n    switch (buttonInnerHTML) {\n      case &quot;w&quot;:\n        var tom1 = new Audio(&quot;./sounds/tom-1.mp3&quot;);\n        tom1.play();\n        break;\n      case &quot;a&quot;:\n        var tom2 = new Audio(&quot;./sounds/tom-2.mp3&quot;);\n        tom2.play();\n        break;\n      case &quot;s&quot;:\n        var tom3 = new Audio(&quot;./sounds/tom-3.mp3&quot;);\n        tom3.play();\n        break;\n      case &quot;d&quot;:\n        var tom4 = new Audio(&quot;./sounds/tom-4.mp3&quot;);\n        tom4.play();\n        break;\n      case &quot;j&quot;:\n        var snare = new Audio(&quot;./sounds/snare.mp3&quot;);\n        snare.play();\n        break;\n      case &quot;k&quot;:\n        var crash = new Audio(&quot;./sounds/crash.mp3&quot;);\n        crash.play();\n        break;\n      case &quot;l&quot;:\n        var kick = new Audio(&quot;./sounds/kick-bass.mp3&quot;);\n        kick.play();\n        break;\n      default:\n        console.log(buttonInnerHTML);\n    }\n  });\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Bare switch skeleton</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">switch (value) {\n  case &quot;a&quot;:\n    // do something\n    break;\n  default:\n    // fallback\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting break, so execution falls through and runs the next case's code too.</li><li>Mismatch between the variable declared in a case and the one on which play() is called (e.g. tom1 declared but audio.play()).</li><li>Case values are strings, so the letters need quotes and match case-sensitively.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The sound files tom-2.mp3 etc. exist in the course starter's sounds folder; the reference lesson 18.03 folder includes only tom-1.mp3, while 18.04 has the full set.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l141", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains JavaScript objects as bundles of related properties, why they beat many separate variables, and how constructor functions with the new keyword mass-produce objects with the same shape. Uses a hotel staff scenario (bellboys, housekeepers) with two build-it-yourself challenges.</p>\n<h3>The problem with separate variables</h3>\n<ul><li>Storing each fact about a person in its own variable (e.g. bellBoy1Name, bellBoy1Age...) works for one person but doesn't scale: each new person needs a new set of variables with invented names.</li><li>The mental model is a table or spreadsheet: one row per property, one column per individual, with the same properties for each.</li></ul>\n<h3>Object literals</h3>\n<ul><li>Create one with var name = { ... }. Curly braces enclose comma-separated property: value pairs.</li><li>Property names follow variable naming conventions: camelCase, not starting with a digit.</li><li>Values can be any type: string, number, boolean, array, even a function.</li><li>Read a value with dot notation: bellBoy1.name. In an alert this becomes a greeting built by string concatenation.</li><li>Different kinds of thing get different properties: a housekeeper might record yearsOfExperience, name and a cleaningRepertoire array.</li><li>Structured data like this is less error-prone than loose variables.</li></ul>\n<h3>Constructor functions</h3>\n<ul><li>Writing out every property for every new object is repetitive. A constructor function is a template (the lecture compares it to a factory) that builds objects for you.</li><li>Convention: the function name starts with a capital letter (BellBoy, Housekeeper), which signals it is a constructor and not an ordinary function.</li><li>Its parameters are the values supplied per object. Inside, assign each to a property of the object being built: this.name = name; this.yearsOfExperience = yearsOfExperience; and so on.</li><li>Create objects by calling it with the new keyword: var houseKeeper1 = new Housekeeper(9, &quot;Tom&quot;, [&quot;Lobby&quot;, &quot;Bedroom&quot;]). Arguments must follow the parameter order.</li><li>The only syntactic difference from calling a normal function is new plus the capitalised name. Omitting new breaks the pattern (this would not refer to a fresh object).</li><li>After creating, access properties via the dot: houseKeeper1.name gives &quot;Tom&quot;.</li><li>Many objects with identical properties but different values can then be created in one line each.</li></ul>\n<h3>Workflow notes</h3>\n<ul><li>The Chrome DevTools Sources tab (snippets) is used to run the code; a hard reload clears earlier definitions.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Object literal</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var housekeeper = {\n  name: &quot;Geovana&quot;,\n  yearsOfExperience: 2,\n  cleaningRepertoire: [&quot;Bathroom&quot;, &quot;Lobby&quot;, &quot;Bedroom&quot;],\n  clean: function () {\n    alert(&quot;Cleaning in progress...&quot;);\n  }\n};\n\nconsole.log(housekeeper.name); // Geovana</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Constructor function and new</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function Housekeeper(name, yearsOfExperience, cleaningRepertoire) {\n  this.name = name;\n  this.yearsOfExperience = yearsOfExperience;\n  this.cleaningRepertoire = cleaningRepertoire;\n  this.clean = function () {\n    alert(&quot;Cleaning in progress...&quot;);\n  };\n}\n\nvar nataly = new Housekeeper(&quot;Nataly&quot;, 5, [&quot;Bathroom&quot;, &quot;Lobby&quot;, &quot;Bedroom&quot;]);\nconsole.log(nataly.name); // Nataly\nnataly.clean();</pre>\n<h2>The task: Housekeeper object and constructor</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty script (DevTools snippet or script.js).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> First build a single object literal, then replace it with a constructor function and create objects from it.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Declare var houseKeeper1 = { ... } with properties of your choice (e.g. yearsOfExperience, name, cleaningRepertoire array).</li><li>Log houseKeeper1.name using dot notation.</li><li>Write a capitalised constructor Housekeeper(...) taking one parameter per property and assigning each to this.</li><li>Delete the literal and create objects with new Housekeeper(...) giving values in parameter order.</li><li>Log a property from the new object to confirm.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Logging the name of an object built via new prints the value you passed in; additional objects take one line each.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function Housekeeper(name, yearsOfExperience, cleaningRepertoire) {\n  this.name = name;\n  this.yearsOfExperience = yearsOfExperience;\n  this.cleaningRepertoire = cleaningRepertoire;\n  this.clean = function () {\n    alert(&quot;Cleaning in progress...&quot;);\n  };\n}\n\nvar nataly = new Housekeeper(&quot;Nataly&quot;, 5, [&quot;Bathroom&quot;, &quot;Lobby&quot;, &quot;Bedroom&quot;]);\nconsole.log(nataly.name);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting new when calling a constructor.</li><li>Passing constructor arguments in a different order from the parameters.</li><li>Starting a constructor name with a lowercase letter (works, but breaks the convention).</li><li>Missing commas between properties in an object literal (no comma after the last one is needed).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The lecture's housekeeper constructor has parameters in the order experience, name, repertoire; the reference repo uses name first. Either is fine if calls match.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Defining a method inside the constructor (this.clean = function...) creates a separate copy per object; prototypes avoid this but are beyond this lesson.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l142", "<p class=\"summary\">Adds methods (functions stored on objects) to object literals and to constructor functions, then links this idea back to the built-in Audio object used in the drum kit. Challenge: add a clean method to the Housekeeper constructor and call it.</p>\n<h3>Methods on an object</h3>\n<ul><li>A method is a function attached to an object. In an object literal, add it as a property whose value is an anonymous function, e.g. moveSuitcase: function () { ... }.</li><li>Call it with dot notation plus parentheses: bellBoy1.moveSuitcase(). Properties and methods are both reached with the dot; the parentheses are what mark a method call.</li><li>In a constructor function, define it with this.moveSuitcase = function () { ... }. Every object built with new then has that method.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge: clean method</h3>\n<ul><li>Inside the Housekeeper constructor add this.clean = function () { alert(&quot;Cleaning in progress...&quot;); }.</li><li>Create an object with new Housekeeper(...) and call houseKeeper1.clean(). An alert should appear.</li><li>Without the parentheses you only reference the function and nothing runs.</li></ul>\n<h3>Connecting to the Audio object</h3>\n<ul><li>new Audio(&quot;file&quot;) follows the same pattern as the housekeeper constructor: the capitalised name is a constructor, the argument is stored as a property (the file location) and the object has methods.</li><li>The hidden Audio constructor can be imagined as setting a file-location property and defining a play() method, which would involve things such as checking the file exists and is audio, then using the audio hardware.</li><li>Code that uses it only needs to construct the object and call the method, which is what the drum kit does with tom1.play().</li><li>Much of DOM scripting is using properties and methods of built-in objects; occasionally you define your own.</li><li>The ideas are abstract; they get familiar through repetition and later projects.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Constructor with a method; call via the dot</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function Housekeeper(name, yearsOfExperience, cleaningRepertoire) {\n  this.name = name;\n  this.yearsOfExperience = yearsOfExperience;\n  this.cleaningRepertoire = cleaningRepertoire;\n  this.clean = function () {\n    console.log(&quot;Cleaning in progress...&quot;);\n  };\n}\n\nvar geovana = new Housekeeper(&quot;Geovana&quot;, 2, [&quot;Bathroom&quot;, &quot;Bedroom&quot;]);\ngeovana.clean();</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Illustrative guess at how Audio might be built (own sketch, not real browser code)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function Audio(fileLocation) {\n  this.fileLocation = fileLocation;\n  this.play = function () {\n    // check the file exists, is a sound, then use audio hardware\n  };\n}\nvar tom1 = new Audio(&quot;sounds/tom-1.mp3&quot;);\ntom1.play();</pre>\n<h2>The task: Add a clean method to Housekeeper</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The Housekeeper constructor from the previous lecture.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Housekeeper objects can clean, shown with an alert or log message.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add this.clean = function () {...} that alerts &quot;Cleaning in progress...&quot;.</li><li>Create a new Housekeeper object with new.</li><li>Call the method with the dot and parentheses.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Calling the method produces the 'Cleaning in progress...' message.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function Housekeeper(name, yearsOfExperience, cleaningRepertoire) {\n  this.name = name;\n  this.yearsOfExperience = yearsOfExperience;\n  this.cleaningRepertoire = cleaningRepertoire;\n  this.clean = function () {\n    console.log(&quot;Cleaning in progress...&quot;);\n  };\n}\n\nvar geovana = new Housekeeper(&quot;Geovana&quot;, 2, [&quot;Bathroom&quot;, &quot;Bedroom&quot;]);\nconsole.log(geovana.clean());</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Calling houseKeeper1.clean without parentheses.</li><li>Assigning the method to a local variable instead of this.clean, so it is not attached to the object.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo logs geovana.clean() inside console.log, which prints the method's own output first and then undefined because the method returns nothing; call geovana.clean() alone.</li><li>Mixing up property access (no parentheses) with method calls (parentheses).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l143", "<p class=\"summary\">Short announcement placed before the keyboard-events lesson. The page body was only an 'Open' link-out, so its text could not be read; it sits between the objects lessons and lecture 144 (keyboard event listeners).</p>\n<h3>Takeaway</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The next lesson in the course order is 144, 'Using Keyboard Event Listeners to Check for Key Presses', which extends the Drum Kit so keys trigger the same sounds as the buttons.</li><li>Content of the note itself was not available in the page, so nothing further is recorded.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l144", "<p class=\"summary\">Extends the drum kit so it reacts to keyboard keys as well as button clicks. Covers attaching a key listener to the whole document, reading the event object's key property, and refactoring the switch statement into a reusable makeSound(key) function.</p>\n<h3>Listening for keyboard input</h3>\n<ul><li>Keyboard events use the same addEventListener method as click handlers: document.addEventListener(type, callback).</li><li>Event type names are all lowercase strings (&quot;keypress&quot;, &quot;keydown&quot;, &quot;keyup&quot;). Check the MDN event list when unsure, since the casing is easy to get wrong.</li><li>A keyboard has no single element to attach to, so the listener goes on the document object. Then the entire page receives key events regardless of what is focused.</li><li>The lecture starts with &quot;keypress&quot; and an alert to prove the listener fires on any key. (Extra) keypress is deprecated; the finished reference code uses &quot;keydown&quot;, which fires for every key including non-character keys, and is the recommended choice.</li></ul>\n<h3>The event object</h3>\n<ul><li>The browser calls the callback with one argument describing what happened. It is conventionally named event or e.</li><li>Logging it with console.log(event) shows a KeyboardEvent whose properties include key, code, keyCode/charCode and more (deprecated ones are still visible in DevTools).</li><li>event.key is the property that matters here: it holds the value of the pressed key as a string, e.g. &quot;w&quot; or &quot;j&quot;.</li><li>Expanding the logged object in the Chrome console is a quick way to discover what data an event gives you.</li></ul>\n<h3>Refactor: one function, two triggers</h3>\n<ul><li>The existing switch statement lived inside the click handler and switched on the clicked button's innerHTML. That ties it to clicks only.</li><li>Move the switch into a named function, makeSound(key), and switch on the key parameter instead.</li><li>Click handler: call makeSound(this.innerHTML) (or via a variable holding the button text).</li><li>Keyboard handler: call makeSound(event.key).</li><li>Both paths end in the same switch, so each letter (w, a, s, d, j, k, l) plays its sound regardless of how it was triggered. The default branch just logs the unmatched key.</li><li>Splitting code into small, named functions like this avoids duplicating logic and keeps 'detect input' separate from 'respond to input'.</li></ul>\n<h3>What comes next</h3>\n<ul><li>Passing a function as an argument (the anonymous function given to addEventListener) is a callback. The next lecture explains callbacks in detail.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Proof-of-concept: listener on the whole document</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.addEventListener(&quot;keypress&quot;, function (event) {\n  console.log(event);   // inspect the KeyboardEvent\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final structure: shared makeSound function (reference repo, trimmed)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var numberOfDrumButtons = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;).length;\n\nfor (var i = 0; i &lt; numberOfDrumButtons; i++) {\n  document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;)[i].addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n    var buttonInnerHTML = this.innerHTML;\n    makeSound(buttonInnerHTML);\n  });\n}\n\ndocument.addEventListener(&quot;keydown&quot;, function (event) {\n  makeSound(event.key);\n});\n\nfunction makeSound(key) {\n  switch (key) {\n    case &quot;w&quot;:\n      var tom1 = new Audio(&quot;./sounds/tom-1.mp3&quot;);\n      tom1.play();\n      break;\n    case &quot;j&quot;:\n      var snare = new Audio(&quot;./sounds/snare.mp3&quot;);\n      snare.play();\n      break;\n    // ...a, s, d, k, l follow the same pattern\n    default:\n      console.log(key);\n  }\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Make the drum kit respond to keys</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Drum kit project from the earlier lectures: click listeners on each .drum button with the sound switch statement written inside the click callback.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create a function that takes a character, runs the switch to play the right sound, and call it from both the button click listener and a new keyboard listener on the document.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add a document-level keyboard listener and log the event to see its properties.</li><li>Find the key property in the event (MDN KeyboardEvent docs).</li><li>Create function makeSound(key) and cut the switch statement into it, switching on key.</li><li>In the click handler call makeSound with the button's innerHTML.</li><li>In the keyboard handler call makeSound(event.key) in place of the console.log.</li><li>Test clicking each drum, then typing w, a, s, d, j, k, l.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each drum plays its sound both when clicked and when its letter is typed.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.addEventListener(&quot;keydown&quot;, function (event) {\n  makeSound(event.key);\n});\n\nfunction makeSound(key) {\n  switch (key) {\n    case &quot;w&quot;: new Audio(&quot;./sounds/tom-1.mp3&quot;).play(); break;\n    // ... other cases\n    default: console.log(key);\n  }\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing event names in camelCase (&quot;keyPress&quot;) so the listener never fires.</li><li>Attaching the keyboard listener to a button rather than document.</li><li>Forgetting to rename the switch variable to the new parameter after moving it.</li><li>Key matching is case sensitive: with Caps Lock or Shift, event.key is &quot;W&quot; and hits the default branch.</li><li>The deprecated keypress event does not fire for some keys; prefer keydown.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l145", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains callbacks: the function handed to a higher-order function such as addEventListener, which the event source calls later and feeds an event object. Builds a simplified fake addEventListener to show where the event object comes from, then steps through it with the debugger.</p>\n<h3>Higher-order function vs callback</h3>\n<ul><li>A higher-order function is one that accepts another function as an argument. addEventListener qualifies because its second argument is a function.</li><li>The function passed in is the callback. It is not run at the point of writing; it is stored and invoked later, when the awaited thing (a click, a key press) occurs.</li><li>So you write the callback, but the object that detected the event is the one that calls it.</li></ul>\n<h3>The event parameter</h3>\n<ul><li>The browser passes details about the event into the callback as its first argument. For a click that is a mouse event with properties such as screen position and the event type; for a key press it is a KeyboardEvent with key.</li><li>The parameter name is your choice: event, e or evt all behave identically. This is the same as any function parameter: a function sayHi(to) and sayHi(name) work the same way when called with &quot;Tom&quot;.</li><li>Experiment in the browser: select an element in DevTools (it becomes $0), then run $0.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function (e) { console.log(e); }) and click it to inspect the logged object.</li></ul>\n<h3>Simulating addEventListener</h3>\n<ul><li>The lecture builds anotherAddEventListener(typeOfEvent, callBack) to show the hidden machinery.</li><li>Inside, when something happens the runtime creates an event object, e.g. { eventType: &quot;keypress&quot;, key: &quot;p&quot;, durationOfkeypress: 2 }.</li><li>It then compares that object's eventType with the type the programmer asked for. Only if they match is callBack(eventThatHappened) executed, passing the object through.</li><li>Calling it with a callback that logs its argument prints the object, just like the real document.addEventListener(&quot;keypress&quot;, fn) prints a KeyboardEvent.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The fake version hard-codes the event; a real browser queues events from the OS and dispatches them through its event loop.</li></ul>\n<h3>Using the debugger to follow it</h3>\n<ul><li>Put the debugger keyword on the line before the call, run it in the console, and step through. Execution enters anotherAddEventListener, creates the event object, evaluates the type comparison, then jumps into the anonymous callback and logs the object.</li><li>Stepping this way makes it visible that the callback runs inside the function it was passed to, with data that function produced.</li><li>Advice: callbacks confuse even experienced developers. Rebuild the fake listener from memory and step through it until the flow is obvious. The concept recurs throughout the course.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Real listener using the event object</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;.title&quot;).addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function (event) {\n  console.log(event);       // the MouseEvent\n  console.log(event.type);  // &quot;click&quot;\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Simplified model of addEventListener (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function anotherAddEventListener(typeOfEvent, callBack) {\n  var eventThatHappened = {\n    eventType: &quot;keypress&quot;,\n    key: &quot;p&quot;,\n    durationOfkeypress: 2,\n  };\n  if (eventThatHappened.eventType === typeOfEvent) {\n    callBack(eventThatHappened);\n  }\n}\n\nanotherAddEventListener(&quot;keypress&quot;, function (event) {\n  console.log(event);\n  console.log(event.eventType);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Parameter naming does not matter</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function sayHi(to) { console.log(&quot;Hello &quot; + to); }\nfunction sayHi2(name) { console.log(&quot;Hello &quot; + name); }\nsayHi(&quot;Tom&quot;);  // same output either way</pre>\n<h2>The task: Recreate the fake addEventListener</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> An empty script with an h1.title in the page (reference: index.html with h1 'Click Me').</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Write your own anotherAddEventListener, call it with a callback, and step through it with the debugger until the call order makes sense.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Define anotherAddEventListener(typeOfEvent, callBack) with a hard-coded eventThatHappened object.</li><li>Add the type comparison and call callBack(eventThatHappened) when equal.</li><li>Call it with &quot;keypress&quot; and a logging callback; add the debugger keyword above the call.</li><li>Step through and watch the callback receive the object.</li><li>Try a non-matching type and confirm the callback never runs.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The event object prints in the console only when the requested type matches.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Thinking you call the callback yourself; the event source does.</li><li>Writing addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, handler()) with parentheses, which runs the function immediately instead of passing it.</li><li>Believing the parameter must be named e or event.</li><li>Reference repo adds an author footer to index.html; ignore it.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l146", "<p class=\"summary\">Finishes the drum kit by flashing the pressed drum. A buttonAnimation(key) function finds the matching element by its class, adds a pressed CSS class, and uses setTimeout to remove it after 100 ms.</p>\n<h3>Shared animation function</h3>\n<ul><li>Both click and keyboard paths already call makeSound with a single character, so a second function buttonAnimation(currentKey) is called beside it in both places.</li><li>Click handler: makeSound(buttonInnerHTML); buttonAnimation(buttonInnerHTML).</li><li>Key handler: makeSound(event.key); buttonAnimation(event.key).</li><li>Passing the same one-character value to both keeps the two input routes consistent.</li></ul>\n<h3>Finding the right button</h3>\n<ul><li>In the HTML each drum button has the shared class drum plus a class equal to its letter (w, a, s, d, j, k, l).</li><li>querySelector uses CSS selector syntax, so a class needs a leading dot: document.querySelector(&quot;.w&quot;).</li><li>Because the key is only the bare letter, build the selector by concatenation: &quot;.&quot; + currentKey. Store the result in activeButton.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A key with no matching element (e.g. &quot;x&quot;) makes querySelector return null, and the next line throws a TypeError. Guard with an if (activeButton) check.</li></ul>\n<h3>Applying the visual state with classList</h3>\n<ul><li>styles.css already defines .pressed, giving the element a lighter box-shadow and 50% opacity.</li><li>Test the idea in the console first: document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).classList.add(&quot;pressed&quot;) applies the style immediately.</li><li>activeButton.classList.add(&quot;pressed&quot;) switches the button into its pressed look.</li><li>On its own, the class never goes away, so the effect only happens once.</li></ul>\n<h3>setTimeout for the reset</h3>\n<ul><li>setTimeout(function, delayInMilliseconds) runs a function once after a delay; it is another callback-taking function.</li><li>First argument: the function to run later (an anonymous function here) which calls activeButton.classList.remove(&quot;pressed&quot;).</li><li>Second argument: the delay in ms. 100 ms (0.1 s) was chosen by experimenting; it reads as a quick flash.</li><li>The code after setTimeout does not wait; the add runs immediately and the remove fires 100 ms later.</li><li>Because the animation lives in the shared function, it works for clicks and keys with no extra code.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">buttonAnimation (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function buttonAnimation(currentKey) {\n  var activeButton = document.querySelector(&quot;.&quot; + currentKey);\n  activeButton.classList.add(&quot;pressed&quot;);\n  setTimeout(function () {\n    activeButton.classList.remove(&quot;pressed&quot;);\n  }, 100);\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Calling both functions from both listeners</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.drum&quot;)[i].addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n  var buttonInnerHTML = this.innerHTML;\n  makeSound(buttonInnerHTML);\n  buttonAnimation(buttonInnerHTML);\n});\n\ndocument.addEventListener(&quot;keydown&quot;, function (event) {\n  makeSound(event.key);\n  buttonAnimation(event.key);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">The provided CSS class</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.pressed {\n  box-shadow: 0 3px 4px 0 #dbedf3;\n  opacity: 0.5;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Add the pressed class to the active button</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Drum kit with makeSound working for clicks and keys; styles.css already contains .pressed.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> When a drum is clicked or its key typed, the drum briefly shows the pressed style, then returns to normal.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create buttonAnimation(currentKey) and call it next to makeSound in both listeners.</li><li>Select the element with document.querySelector(&quot;.&quot; + currentKey) and store it in activeButton.</li><li>Challenge: add the pressed class using classList.add.</li><li>Look up setTimeout and use it to remove the class after about 100 ms.</li><li>Test with clicks and with each of the seven keys.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each drum dims with a small shadow for a tenth of a second whenever triggered.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function buttonAnimation(currentKey) {\n  var activeButton = document.querySelector(&quot;.&quot; + currentKey);\n  activeButton.classList.add(&quot;pressed&quot;);\n  setTimeout(function () {\n    activeButton.classList.remove(&quot;pressed&quot;);\n  }, 100);\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the dot when building the selector (querySelector(currentKey)).</li><li>Delay passed in seconds rather than milliseconds.</li><li>Calling the remove immediately outside setTimeout, so the class vanishes before painting.</li><li>Pressing a non-drum key throws an error because querySelector returns null.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l147", "<p class=\"summary\">Resource lecture offering the finished drum kit project as a download so the learner can compare it with their own build.</p>\n<h3>Using the download</h3>\n<ul><li>Use the Resources button on this lecture to get the completed drum kit (HTML, CSS, JS, images and sounds).</li><li>Compare it against your own version only after attempting the build, especially script.js (makeSound, buttonAnimation) and the .pressed rule in styles.css.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's version of this project lives in the 18.09 folder and has the keydown listener, makeSound and buttonAnimation.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l148", "<p class=\"summary\">A motivational tip for learners who feel they are not progressing despite weeks of study.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Feeling that you know little is normal at any stage; nobody knows everything in even one technology, so learn what your goals need after the fundamentals.</li><li>Perceived difficulty stays roughly constant because the topics keep getting harder as you improve, which hides real progress.</li><li>To measure progress, revisit the Q&amp;A from early sections and try answering beginners' questions. Being able to answer them shows how far you have come.</li><li>Answering those questions also reinforces your own knowledge.</li></ul>");
